thank you napb for inviting me to  present today my name is nicholas  morales and i'm a fourth year phd  student  majoring in plant breeding and genetics  i'm also minoring in statistics with a  focus on data science as well as  minoring in environmental information  science  my co-advisors are dr kelly robbins and  dr lucas mueller  today i will present my research  detecting genetic and local  environmental effects  using high-throughput aerial imagery  my research aims are essentially  two-fold  first i want to understand the genotype  by environment for g-by-e effects  present in the collected aerial image  high throughput phenotypes  specifically i'm looking at g by e rage  originating from local field effects  such as from soil heterogeneity  from soil elevation soil curvature  or composition  as we collect more data across years and  field locations we can then look at g by  e across these environments  the high throughput phenotypes i'm  currently looking at are simple  vegetation indices such as ndvi  however from the collected aerial images  we can potentially extract more  informative and predictive features  other than vegetation indices  ndvi was a good starting point given  research indicating it is a heritable  phenotype indicative of plant health  secondly  i am to monitor the stability of those  high throughput phenotypes for genotypes  across environments and across the  growing season  i intend to use reaction norm models to  look at genotypic deviations  across environments in the growing  season  to probe these questions i'm flying an  unoccupied aerial vehicle or uav mounted  with a micasense 5-channel  multi-spectral camera  this camera captures images in the blue  green red  far red and near infrared spectra as you  can see here on the right  it has been a great learning experience  working the logistics of flying  throughout the growing season  from getting an faa license to  transporting and maintaining the uav  the uav flies a pre-programmed path over  the field at an elevation of 25 to 35  meters  i'm flying over  hybrid maze field experiments  in the genomes to fields program  over the over the past uh three years  now  i'm also flying over alfalfa field  experiments but i won't show this here  today  i try to fly twice per week over the  entire course of the growing season  then tither for phenotypes are extracted  for each plot for each time point  image breed is a web database software  i've developed that allows drawing plot  polygon templates  such as the one  [Music]  shown on the right  in blue and red  the plot images are then saved along  with the  extracted high throughput phenotypes  i'm very proud of the image breed tools  because they are an end-to-end platform  for processing aerial imagery for plant  breeding  images coming off the camera can be  directly uploaded to image breed and  high throughput phenotypes extracted for  each plot  imagery builds on breed base which is a  database  we developed in lupus mueller's group  and is currently still in development  this first public publication roughly  describes the aerial image tools  and processing  and the second publication describes in  detail how the high throughput  how the high density genotyping data  is stored in the system  in short image read  connects the aerial images and their  high throughput phenotypes  to the field experiment  and to the high density genotype data  for the hybrids  as part of the genomes to fields program  we have high density  genomic data  giving close to a million  marker genotypes for the mace hybrids  we've tested  and this is loaded into image breed  allowing genomic relationship  matrices to be calculated  to illustrate here we can see six time  points flown over a hybrid maze  experiment  in 2019  this experiment was 612 hybrids planted  over 800  plots  again in blue and red you can see the  plot polygons that were assigned in  imagery  vegetation indices were extracted  namely ndvi  for each plot and for each time point  the collected high throughput phenotypes  can then  [Music]  allow us to see different high  see the performance of different hybrids  over the growing season  this chart shows mean ndvi values for 15  maze hybrids  across 12 time points  we can see trends across emergence  flowering and ultimately senescence  we can see ndvi show stable trends for  the hybrids  giving us assurances in our aerial  imaging methods  my research is then  separating genetic effects from  environmental effects present in those  high throughput phenotypes using linear  mixed models  the random additive genetic effects are  modeled using genomic relationships  among the hybrids while the random  environmental effects are found using  spatial corrections  and random regressions  to model the spatial correction i'm  looking at two-dimensional splines as  well as separable autoregressive  processes ar1 by ar1  these linear  these spatial effects draw information  based on the row and column positions  of the plots in the field  the random regression is distinct from  the spatial corrections in that it draws  information from the repeated nature of  the phenotype  over the growing season  the random regression has come  computational advantages over the  spatial correction  models and allows computing  a continuous covariance function across  the entire growing season the random  regression also allows more flexibility  in defining  [Music]  the covariance structure between plots  the results from all of these linear  mixed models are of the same form and  here i am showing  representative figures  they give the additive genetic effects  for each hybrid across time as shown on  the left where each line is a hybrid  across time  and they give the local environmental  effects for each plot  across time as shown on the right  each one of these heat maps is the  environmental effect present  across the field at a given time point  and i'm showing six time points  here we can consistently identify a  poorly  performing region near the middle of the  field  for instance  given that these  linear mixed models  are essentially unsupervised  and and that we don't know the true  genetic or environmental effects present  in the higher throughput phenotypes  i'm using two approaches to compare the  models  the first approach is to use simulation  and involve simulating  an environmental effect  across the time points and then seeing  how well the linear mixed models can  recover that effect  here on the left we see a  linearly simulated effect that is  constant through time  over the six time points on the right we  can see how well various tested linear  mixed models can recover that effect  with this showing  that the  the prediction accuracy of various  models and scenarios  we can see in this case that the  separable autoregressive process and the  two-dimensional spline  process can recover this effect well  here we see a second simulation where  the simulated effect follows a more  complex gradient  and is constant through time  on the right we see that the random  regression does well at recovering this  effect  there are many ways to simulate  and i'm looking at six different  processes so i'm not showing four to  four other processes here  my results indicate that  specific linear mix models are better  than others are recovering specific  types of effects  in other words not all  linear mixed models are created equally  and so  air limitary may allow us to classify  the field  based on the effects present in that  field  thereby we can know the best type of  linear mixed model to use  to account for the local environmental  effects  and to make better  genotypic selections  the second approach for comparing  these linear mixed models is to compare  with soil data we collected  so we conducted an em-38 soil survey in  2019 and in  2021  and um here i'm showing the 2019 data  uh so you can see on the left um soil  electrical conductance or ec  and then on the right soil elevation or  altitude  and i  and these values were accreting  interpolated over the field from the  soil survey  i then calculated numerical derivatives  of the ec and altitude  to look at curvature in the field so d e  c is the first  derivative of v c well d alt is the  first derivative of  altitude these are numerical two two  dimensional derivatives  and um these results indicate  that um soil elevation curvature  explains the the linear mixed model  environmental effects  well but there's more research to be  done  and i would like to thank everyone in  the robbins and mueller labs as well as  my funding sources  and if you have any questions please  feel free to contact me thank you
 

